Dominic Raab: Government cannot apologize for decision to isolate Spain
Dominic Raab defended the government's sudden decision to reimpose isolation measures on Spain.
The ministers announced on Saturday that travelers who fail to return from Spain and its islands before midnight will be required to self-isolate for 14 days.
Spain has been on the list of safe travel corridors on Downing Street, however, the recent surge in coronavirus cases has raised public concerns about the second wave of the outbreak.
Defending the sudden re-imposition of travel restrictions on Sunday, the Foreign Minister said the move was a real-time response to the surge in reported cases of infection in the region.
He said that the situation had been assessed the previous afternoon and that we had made a decision as soon as possible.
He added: “We cannot apologize for this.”
“We must be capable of taking swift and decisive action, especially in cases involving our homeland or Spain or specific countries, and we must act.”
Otherwise, the UK will face another risk of infection, triggering a second wave of outbreaks, and then re-impose a lockdown.
The Minister of Transport faces mandatory quarantine just like vacationers heading to Spain.
Following two consecutive days of reporting more than 900 new coronavirus cases per day in Spain, the government made this decision.
The Foreign Minister acknowledged that many tourists did not have time to cancel or change their travel plans.
He said: "Well, I know this is a very unpleasant decision for those who are in Spain or who have been thinking about going to Spain, but we must be able to take swift and decisive action to protect Britain".
We have achieved a lot in terms of reducing the number of cases and preventing the response to the epidemic.
Shadow Health Minister Jonathan Ashworth has condemned the government's handling of the issue as unjustified.
He said that the sudden decision on Downing Street left the holidaymakers both confused and frustrated.
The Labour MP told Sophy Ridge: "Well, I understand why they made this decision, but they've made a decision like this in the last 24 hours without a chapter".
Those who are on holiday in Spain at this time will be confused and frustrated, and those who plan to go on holiday to islands such as Spain and Tenerife will be confused, not knowing whether their employers will accept their two-week quarantine.
“The government is just saying ‘we hope employers will cooperate.’”
"They've made this decision in the last 24 hours without law."
According to CDC, salmonella is a bacterial food-borne disease that causes 1.35 million infections each year, 26,500 hospitalizations and 420 deaths.
Common symptoms after infection with Salmonella include diarrhea, fever, and stomach cramps, which may begin to appear six hours to six days after exposure to the bacteria.
Symptoms usually last for four to seven days, and most people recover without treatment, but some cases require hospitalization.
The California dog park couple was assaulted after eating lunch without masks (video) - RT TV's U.S. News
First the mask shame, then the punches and kicks.
A couple in California had this happen when they were having lunch with their dog at a dog park. A woman sprayed pepper spray at them because they were not wearing masks while eating.
Ash O'Brien and her husband Jarett Kelley said they had lunch with their three-month-old dog at Dusty Rhodes Dog Park in San Diego on Thursday.
When the couple took off their masks to eat, an “old lady who came out of nowhere” came along and called them “dumbs” because they were not wearing masks, although they said “how to eat in masks”.
O'Brien asked the woman to leave the park and stop her harassment, when she sprayed pepper spray on both.
The video, which was filmed by viewers, begins with Kelley fighting a woman for a spray bottle, for which he was sprayed in the face.
After the attackers left with their dogs, O'Brien can be heard crying in the background.
O'Brien posted the video on Facebook and told ABC 10 that she wanted the woman to go to jail.
Masks are mandatory throughout California, even where the air is fresh.
People can only take off their masks during designated times, such as when eating.
However, the state's new coronavirus pandemic prevention guidelines do not mention that bystanders can use non-lethal weapons to urge enforcement of this provision.
Even in the current era of masked humiliation, it is a crime punishable by a $1,000 fine or up to three years in state prison.
An Indore boy who sold eggs was overthrown for refusing to ride a car and was given a 100 rupee stipend for an apartment and free education
It was reported that on Thursday, Paras Raykar's car, which was full of eggs, was overturned by municipal officials in Indore.
The story of a 13-year-old boy who failed to sell eggs and had his car overturned by municipal officials spread on social media, and two days later, the boy from Indore received support from all over the country to help them recover their losses and restart their business.
The boy's family also said that politicians including Nationalist Congress Party chief Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal had contacted them and offered help as well.
The boy's family was also given an apartment and received a commitment to pay for the children's education.
On Thursday, it was reported that Paras Raykar's egg-selling pickup truck was overturned by Indore municipal officials, and the city implemented measures to switch shops from left to right as the epidemic raged.
In a video, the boy said that in the morning, officials warned him that if he didn't pay 100 rupees, he would overturn his van, and the video quickly spread on social media.
The boy said that after he refused to hand over 100 rupees, those officials overturned his little car and smashed all the eggs.
The boy also said in the video that due to the impact of the epidemic, the daily sales of eggs have declined, and the loss of the eggs is even worse for him.
Since Thursday, the boy's family has received a large amount of support from across the country, with many providing cash or goodwill assistance.
Local people also extended a helping hand to this family.
Former Chief Minister Digvijaya Singh funded them and helped the children enroll in a college; People's Party MP Ramesh Mendola also assured that a flat would be provided to the boy's family under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
The Indore Journalists Club also provided food and money for the boys.
The office of Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also extended help.
Paras' grandfather said, "We have received support from many people all over the country."
Mr. Ramesh Mendola of the People's Party guarantees that we will be provided with an apartment under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
He provided a bicycle and a grant of 2,500 rupees.
Digvijaya Singh contributed 10,000 rupees and also promised to look after the education of the two children.
Rahul Gandhi's staff also called us to offer help.
(Representative) A female employee of Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also called us and helped us.
Jyothiraditya Scindia also gave his support,”.
The Home Secretary Priti Patel has asked Twitter and Instagram to explain the inappropriate content posted by Wiley.
Since Twitter and Instagram failed to remove inappropriate content posted by Wiley in a timely manner, Priti Patel asked the two platforms to provide an explanation.
Police are currently investigating a series of anti-Semitic comments posted on social media by the Grime singer.
The Minister of Home Affairs tweeted on Sunday: "Wiley's anti-Semitic content is deeply offensive."
Twitter and Instagram have not removed the content in a timely manner, and I have asked them for a full explanation.
Social media companies must act faster to remove these shocking hate speech from their platforms.
He criticized Twitter for ignoring antisemitism because Wiley's tweets remained visible 12 hours after they were posted.
Subsequently, the website deleted multiple tweets and banned the account of the 41-year-old singer for seven days.
Twitter has previously said it has temporarily shut down Wiley's account for violating its hate speech policy, while Facebook has said it does not allow hate speech on Instagram.
On Saturday, the NYPD said, "The NYPD is extremely concerned about all reports of anti-Semitism. We are currently evaluating the relevant materials."
Meanwhile, Wiley's A-List agency has also severed all ties with the celebrity.
The ex-agent of the singer, John Woolf, who is Jewish, wrote on Twitter: “Given the anti-Semitic content Wiley posted today, we @A_ListMGMT have severed all ties with him. There is no place for anti-Semitism in this society.”
Earlier, he had indicated that he neither supported nor condoned Wiley's statements, but would communicate privately and “teach him”.
However, an Instagram video shows that Wiley appears to have severed ties with its brokerage firm.
Other lawmakers also criticized Twitter for not deleting it in time.
Labour MP Jess Phillips said on Saturday: "Oh, I just saw what Wiley posted. Why does @Twitter keep such blatant anti-Semitism and hate speech? It involves a lot of dangerous views: Jews get what you don't get, they control everything, they think they're better, etc. Such content is dangerous and should be deleted".
MP Neil Coyle added: "His brokerage company seems to be responding faster than @Twitter, and I reiterate that legislation should be passed -- including the Cyber Harm Act -- to ensure that social media platforms are not used to spread hate.
John Mann, an independent adviser to the government on anti-Semitism, said the tweets accurately reflect the need for changes to the law to bring about a reasonable accountability.
The anti-Semitic movement also called on the police to investigate Wiley's content and called for his social media account to be shut down in case of another spray of anti-Semitic poisoning.
Wiley, whose real name is Richard Cowie, is known as the "Grime Music Father" and was awarded an MBE in 2018 for his contributions to music.
Kudlow says the next New Crown rescue plan has more stuck checks and extended suspension of deportation
White House economic adviser Larry Kudlow pledged on Sunday that the federal government would extend the suspension of evictions and issue a new round of bail-in checks under the next coronavirus outbreak rescue plan.
The week before Kudlow made the pledge, the White House's payroll tax cuts were rejected by Congress.
Residents of Massart Mallawi village declined to comment on the incident, only expressing condolences to the families of the children.
Khalid Darwish, adviser to the National Commission for Women and Children, said in October that about 93% of Egyptian children aged 1 to 14 had been subjected to violence.
He added that prosecution is one of the most important means to eliminate child abuse, and emphasized the need for enhanced cooperation between the Committee and the prosecutor's office in this regard.
A research conducted by the National Committee on Women and Children found that children can also be subjected to violence even in places where they should be relatively safe, such as their homes, schools, or communities, with perpetrators including those who are supposed to care for them—such as parents or teachers.
AP criticizes White House Press Secretary Kayleigh McEnany's "political" briefing, but journalists continue to ask political questions – RT TV America News
The Associated Press accused White House press secretary Kayleigh McEnany of making press conferences more political than journalistic, causing the media to worry, but obviously forgetting that these were issues of journalists' choice.
The largest news agency in the United States, The Associated Press, wrote on Saturday that the White House press conference may have broken the tradition, as McEnany turned the press conference into a hotbed for brief high-profile propaganda and attacks on the media for presidential re-election, while information on what the government is doing is relatively scarce.
The last review is understandable as very little new information was disclosed at the press conference.
What the Associated Press did not mention is that almost no one asked for any new information at the press conference.
Journalists often use White House press secretaries to draw attention to themselves, and Jim Acosta of CNN took this tactic to the extreme.
They go beyond the common antagonistic relationship between the media and the government and repeatedly ask each other questions about traps, only to question President Donald Trump.
On some occasions, they make themselves news, for example, in response to Trump's refutation, Weijia Jiang, a BBC news reporter, made a less subtle hint when she asked China: "Mr. Liu, why do you say this to me in particular?"
The July 6 conference is a good example of how it is no longer a source of effective information for the American people.
Earlier this morning, Trump tweeted that NASCAR viewership had declined because of false reports of alleged hate crimes against driver Bubba Wallace and the decision to ban the display of the Confederate flag at the race.
Just as expected, one by one, reporters asked McEnany questions related to this tweet, trying to get her to openly admit that Trump supports the Confederate flag.
McEnany didn't let reporters down, saying it would hurt Trump's interests, but she repeatedly responded that the president had no stance on the Confederate flag, his tweet was about NASCAR fans being mischaracterized as racists.
The Associated Press reports that she sounded "flushed with anger" during the press conference.
The Associated Press story echoes the column published earlier this month by American Broadcasting Corporation journalist Jonathan Karl in The Washington Post, who is also President of the White House Correspondents Association.
Karl said that denying reality and using the White House podium for purely political purposes violates public trust.
McEnany took over as press secretary in April and hosted her first press conference on May 1. Her wit and fighting style led some Trump supporters to view her as a hero.
Before she was appointed, the position of press officer had been vacant for more than a year.
As with other aspects of Washington's political life, the relationship between the White House press secretary and the media under Trump has changed dramatically.
This change came from both sides, and had already happened before McEnany arrived.
At a White House Correspondent's Dinner in April 2018, comedian Michelle Wolf sent vicious torture to former press secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders, which even MSNBC host Mika Brzezinski called shocking.
AP White House correspondent Jill Colvin asked McEnany at her first press conference, "Can you promise never to lie to us from that podium?"
Fox News political analyst Brit Hume, a seasoned journalist with over 50 years of experience, who served as ABC's chief White House correspondent from 1989 to 1996, says that reporters now exhibit "aggressive behavior" during press conferences because they look down on Trump.
Speaking on Fox's Tucker Carlson Tonight Tonight show this week, he said it was because reporters focused on singing back to Trump and ignoring the collection of policy news.
"This is not just a game of cat and mouse," Hume said in discussing the media's intended purpose.
Our goal is not just to keep government officials from coming down, but to get information.
California protesters burn courthouse
Oakland (California) – Oakland police said that late Saturday night, peaceful protests in California turned into violent incidents. Protesters set fire to a courthouse, smashed police stations, and attacked several officers.
Officials in Auckland tweeted that protesters damaged windows, sprayed graffiti, and fired fireworks and used lasers on police officers.
Some called for peace on Twitter, with tweets requesting organizers to "help us provide safe spaces and locations for the protesters."
The demonstration began on Saturday night, with organizations participating in the protests including the Wall of Fire (similar to related organizations in the city of Portland, Oregon). At the same time, the protesters clashed with police officers deployed to the Federal Court responsible for security work.
Since the May 25 George Floyd incident in Minneapolis, President Donald Trump has sent federal police to suppress demonstrations every night.
Initially, protesters held peaceful demonstrations in Auckland, but they later turned into violent clashes in the evening.
Around 11:30 PM, police declared the demonstration an "illegal assembly" and demanded that the demonstrators disperse.
According to news reports, a fire broke out at the Alameda County Superior Court, but the fire was brought under control in a short time.
According to police, the protesters once smashed glass and made racially discriminatory insults against residents.
In a photo posted on Twitter by the relevant authorities, the glass in the police station building was shattered and the walls were covered with graffiti.
High-ranking UN officials cancelled visits to Pakistan due to flights
ISLAMBURG (AFP) The incoming president of the United Nations General Assembly announced on Sunday that he had postponed his visit to Pakistan due to technical problems.
Last month, Turkish diplomat Volkan Bozkir was successfully elected president of the 75th session of the United Nations General Assembly, which was scheduled to arrive in Islamabad on Sunday.
However, he tweeted that plans to visit Pakistan's capital had to be postponed.
Pakistan's Foreign Minister Shah Mahmood Qureshi tweeted in response to Bozkir, saying that he warmly welcomes you to visit Pakistan as soon as possible for a constructive and fruitful visit.
Just one day ago, Qureshi enthusiastically released the news of the visit, expressing the hope that discussions would be held with Bozkir on a range of issues, including Kashmir, between Pakistan and neighbouring India, both of which claim sovereignty over the entire territory of Kashmir.
Since independence from British rule in 1947, there have been three wars between Pakistan and India, both involving the disputed region.
The two sides have frequently engaged in firefights in Kashmir, resulting in casualties among military personnel and civilians.
Pakistan hopes that the UN will adopt a resolution allowing people living in the Himalayan region to decide whether to merge with India or Pakistan or become independent, thus pushing for a solution to the Kashmir issue.
Manchester United secured a place in the Champions League, and Paul Pogba hinted at staying with the team.
After the Red Devils were locked in the top four, Manchester United star Paul Pogba hinted that he would remain with the team this summer.
A 2-0 win over Leicester City meant Manchester United ended up in third place ahead of Frank Lampard's Chelsea team.
Before their 12th game, Leicester City were still leading Manchester United by 14 points, but the Red Devils made a strong run to reach the quarter-finals.
Pogba has asked to leave the team twice in the last two years, so fans predict Pogba will leave the team this summer.
However, the pandemic means that few teams will be spending heavily this summer, and there is no buyer who can afford both the transfer fee and the wages needed to lure Pogba away from Old Trafford.
Making such a statement has been helpful for Pogba in the past few weeks, as his performance and body language have improved.
After the team won 2-0, the Frenchman posted on Instagram, implying that he would stay with the team.
Although Pogba may be merely celebrating the team's return to the European Championship, many fans understand that he strongly implies that he will continue to remain at the top of the standings with Europe's top players.
Pogba's current contract has one year remaining, but the club can extend it for another year, meaning he would actually be under contract for two more years.
Manchester United are eager to extend Pogba's contract, hoping to clarify his situation before next season.
As for Pogba, he was very comfortable with the team, and the signing of Bruno Fernandes made him more like a tiger.
The Portuguese midfielder became the core of Old Trafford, and his arrival enabled Pogba to drive straight into deeper midfield positions and also improved his mood.
Aubameyang helps Arsenal send Watford to relegation from the Premier League.
Some Watford players knelt to the ground, while others turned their heads away and left the field without looking back.
The whistle of Watford's return to the Premier League for the fifth year has just been sounded, but no one can say that Watford did not fight at a time when it was necessary for Watford to pursue Arsenal with all his might.
In fact, it was Watford's hardest-fought game in five years, and with three points to spare, Watford almost reversed the battlefield, ultimately losing to Arsenal.
16 minutes before the end of the game, the goal was scored by Emiliano Martínez after Danny Welbeck had kicked the ball, otherwise Welbeck would have taken the third point for Watford, overtaking Arsenal and retaining the Premier League title.
But in the end, Watford failed to achieve this, and their caretaker manager Hayden Mullins stated that if their previous form had been as good as it was in this match, he would not have had to exert himself so much.
Throughout the season, Watford struggled, and owner Pozzo's management of the team was also chaotic.
A failed campaign may not destroy an empire, but the Pozzo family cannot lay the foundations for Watford to secure its return to the Premier League.
Waterford's frequent change of sails has become an inescapable fate.
In summary, the next lookout will be the 12th time in eight years, but the fresh blood that the lookout can inject is very limited.
Captain Troy Deeney faces relegation with the team, although he can still play in the UAE Super League.
Some players may also choose to leave, such as the attacker Ismaïla Sarr, once again saying that the September championship is unbelievable, but preserving power is the first task at this stage.
Arsenal's head coach Mikel Arteta said that the two sides' odds of winning were unlikely, which made the match even better.
Arteta also said that Watford's predecessor had been cheating and soon changed the word to the football term to try to keep him safe.
Arsenal saying this is not without justification; Arsenal is ahead in the score, and Watford must press relentlessly to catch up.
Watford lost a goal in the opening game.
In the first 40 seconds of the game, Craig Dawson accidentally tripped Alexandre Lacazette, and after a lengthy verification by VAR lasting approximately 3 minutes, a penalty was awarded. Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang scored the penalty kick.
Watford pressed hard, and Sarr quickly counter-attacked with the ball on the right side, with Roberto Pereyra and Troy Deeney providing cover as they closed in on the goal.
A former Labour member told the Daily Mail that even if Starmer expelled Corbyn from the party, they would still sue him.
What I mean is that he really made the Labor Party competitive again.
He still has a long way to go in politics, but he's got a very impressive start, and he's gotten to the point where a lot of people who left the Labour Party, who are no longer Labour voters, come back to identify with the Labour Party and see him as a serious person.
Blair indeed fears that the Labour Party is still seen as a party that only raises taxes.
He said: "Well, in my experience, if you're a member of the Labour Party, you have to be very careful about taxes, because the public isn't sitting there worrying about whether you're going to raise taxes, they're more likely to be worried about whether you're going to raise taxes, so you have to be careful about that mentality".
The coronavirus will permanently alter the world economy (Opinion)
Just as security measures were everywhere after 9/11, virus screening could also become a part of our lives.
It is imperative that the necessary infrastructure be invested to respond to future virus testing.
If coronavirus immunization is only a short-term effect, this investment can provide security for economic operations.
During the pandemic, many economies implemented policies similar to Germany’s “short-time work” (Kurzarbeit) subsidy.
Under this policy, workers' working hours and wages will be reduced accordingly, and underpaid wages will be partially subsidized by the government.
The initiative maintains the links between businesses and workers, thus laying a solid foundation for a rapid economic recovery.
Improving policy implementation is also important; these policies need to become permanent components of economic recovery tools.
Remote work is likely to become increasingly common.
There is evidence that working from home can be just as efficient as working in an office.
However, many companies are still reluctant to accept the working methods of telecommuting.
This approach is likely to continue now that telecommuting has been widely tried and has yielded good results.
With the further development of e-commerce and the increasing adoption of remote healthcare, video conferencing, online teaching and financial technology, the epidemic crisis has also accelerated the pace of digital transformation.
Companies involved in cross-border supply chains are struggling with various shortages and bottlenecks.
We may see many such companies shifting production back to their home countries.
Finance Minister Fiona Hyslop believes that the coronavirus outbreak has had a catastrophic impact on various activities.
Restrictive measures to prevent the spread of the coronavirus have affected sporting events such as festivals and Highland Games in Scotland.
Ms. Hyslop said, “While we have made a positive step, such as the resumption of drive-in theaters, most industries will not be able to operate for some time.”
We have been engaging in close and constructive collaborations with industry organizers, who have come up with creative solutions for how to operate safely given their respective challenges.
In addition to direct funding, some of the funds will be used to promote innovation and sustain long-term recovery of the event activities.”
Event Industry Advisory Group believes that the industry is one of the earliest areas to be restricted and will be one of the last to be completely lifted.
Peter Duthie, the group's chairman, also serves as the executive chairman of the Glasgow Stock Exchange, and said the event generated almost £6 billion in revenue for Scotland each year.
He said, “It is necessary for the government to provide additional funds to the industry, and widespread support has been garnered from event organizers and many related supply chain enterprises that have suffered severe economic losses due to prolonged lockdown restrictions.”
Shivraj Singh Chouhan has been diagnosed with coronary pneumonia
Chief Minister of Madhya Pradesh, Shivraj Singh Chouhan, confirmed Saturday that his coronavirus test results were positive and said in a tweet posted Sunday morning that he was “fine” and praised those on the front lines of the fight against the pandemic.
The Chief Minister also reiterated the warning he issued yesterday, urging people to follow the pandemic guidelines, such as social distancing and wearing masks.
“Friends, I'm fine. The anti-epidemic fighters are to be commended for risking their lives and selfless dedication. I salute all the anti-epidemic fighters in the country.” Chouhan posted a tweet at Chirayu Hospital in Bhopal, saying that he was admitted to hospital yesterday.
“Keeping a distance of two meters, washing hands frequently, and wearing masks – these are our most effective weapons against the coronavirus,” he said, “I hope everyone can use these weapons to protect themselves and their families.”
Chouhan finally added: "Everyone be careful, I hope you are all well. This is what I pray to God".
On Saturday, Chouhan tweeted that he tested positive for the virus, although he had done his best to prevent it from happening, warning that a little negligence could lead to coronavirus infection.
Chouhan tweeted this morning that while there is still a threat of the virus, there is no need for people to be afraid of the virus, and urged those with symptoms to get tested.
He said, "Well, if you're infected with the virus, don't be afraid. Don't hide your symptoms, tell your doctor immediately and then start treatment.
There have been nearly 27,000 confirmed cases in Madhya Pradesh, of which 7,600 were active cases and 799 deaths related to the new coronary virus.
India has seen a surge in confirmed cases in the last few weeks, with 45,000 new confirmed cases per day since July 23.
The total number of confirmed cases of COVID-19 is rapidly approaching 1.4 million, with over 467,000 active cases and over 32,000 deaths linked to the virus.
And what could be more suitable than a rock from Mars for studying rocks on Mars? says Professor Caroline Smith, curator of the Meteorite Gallery at the Natural History Museum.
Smith is also a member of the “Mars 2020” science team and adds that scientists believe the rocks they returned were from Mars.
The tiny bubbles within the meteorite are identical to the composition of Mars' atmosphere, which is why we are certain that this rock came from there.
It is believed that this Martian meteorite was created about 600,000 to 700,000 years ago on the impact of an asteroid or comet on Mars, with debris scattered into space.
One of the fragments swept through the solar system and eventually landed on Earth.
The meteorite, now known as SAU 008, was discovered in Oman in 1999 and has been kept by the Natural History Museum since then.
A high-precision laser emitter, named Sherloc, will be used to decipher the chemical composition of Martian rocks and determine whether they contain organic matter that could indicate that there was or is still life on Mars.
Adding a piece of SAU 008 was to ensure maximum accuracy in completing this operation.
Smith added: Well, we chose this rock especially because it fits the chemical composition and it's a very hard rock.
One of the Martian meteorites we found is very fragile. But this one is as hard as an old boot.
When the InSight finds rocks of research value, it will store them on the Martian surface.
Then, these rocks will be recovered by subsequent robots sent to Mars and launched back to Earth for research and analysis.
A man was found shot dead in Mambourin near Werribi
Last night, a man in western Melbourne was shot and rushed to the hospital for emergency treatment.
Around 8 p.m., police arrived in Mambourin, near Werribee, and found a man in his 20s in Yard Street with multiple gunshot wounds suspiciously in his lower half.
The man was taken to hospital for treatment and is not in danger of death.
The police said they were investigating this case.
Oliver Spencer's Sunday: "I don't dress on Sundays and wear pyjamas instead"
How did your Sunday start, please?
I stumbled down the stairs and was knocked down by dogs on the way to make tea for my wife.
After disembarking from the ferry, we drove up to the A3 motorway and headed back to London to prepare for the start of our workday. Upon arriving home, the children went to bed while I came back after walking my dog and had another bowl of cereal.
I was happy to return to London from the Isle of Wight, because I knew that I would not have a home on the Isle of Wight if I hadn't.
Kenyan police in Garissa arrested for shooting incident
Two Kenyan police officers have been arrested on suspicion of causing two deaths in a shooting incident.
The shooting took place on Saturday at the Soko Ng'ombe market in eastern Garisa County, when the market was protesting a suspected murder case against a man.
The two victims were Aden Abdi Madobe and Muhiyadin Adow Shibin.
The Kenyan Police Inspectorate has launched an investigation into the shooting.
The investigation process includes forensic analysis of the involved firearms.
Aden Duale, a Garissa lawmaker, called the incident "heinous" and condemned "police brutality" in a post on Facebook.
Several activists have also expressed concern about the police's law enforcement actions during the pandemic.
According to data from Amnesty International, since March, Kenyan police have caused the deaths of 21 people for non-compliance with curfews and mask-wearing requirements.
Early this month, the police dropped tear gas in the capital, Nairobi, and arrested several demonstrators who participated in the annual Saba Saba march, which originated 30 years ago in a protest against former President Daniel arap Moi's dictatorship.
A front-line reporter says that protests this year were more radical due to the public anger sparked by the police crackdowns during the pandemic lockdown.
Security upgraded outside the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China
On Sunday, security outside the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China, was upgraded and U.S. personnel were ready to evacuate.
Three moving trucks entered the building, with uniformed police and plainclothes police lined up along both sides of the street, and metal barriers set up along the roadside.
China's Global Times editor posted a tweet stating that the US Consulate General in Chengdu was required to close within 72 hours, i.e., at 10 am on Monday, July 27th (0200 GMT).
China accuses some personnel at the US Consulate General in Chengdu of engaging in activities inconsistent with their status.
The spokesperson for the Chinese Foreign Ministry, Wang Wenbin, did not provide any details and merely stated that such activities interfere with China's internal affairs and harm China's security interests.
China demanded on Friday that the U.S. consulate in Chengdu be closed, as a countermeasure to the U.S. demand to close China's consulate in Houston, Texas.
Washington wants China to close its Consulate General in Houston "to protect US intellectual property and private information of Americans".
After the Chinese Consulate General in Houston closed on Friday, a group of individuals resembling American officials entered the facility through the back door.
Xi Jinping said on Saturday that the act was a serious violation of international law and bilateral agreements, and that China would respond, but did not specify specific measures.
The relative closure of consulates has further exacerbated tensions between the two countries over trade and industrial espionage and human rights issues.
The pranksters provide wrecking tools, and in the zoo, baboons wave all sorts of implements.
The primates of the Northley Wildlife Sanctuary in Merseyside are notorious for aiming at cars parked in fences, raincoats or rear-view mirrors.
People saw apes at Northleigh Wildlife Park in Merseyside brandishing small knives, screwdrivers, and chainsaws.
Staffers fear that pranksters will provide these annoying primates with weapons and they will pounce on stopped cars, smashing windshield wipers or rear-view mirrors.
Now, staff are worried that the tourists will be given deadly weapons to open their jokes, which will increase their aggression, and some have even seen the turmoil overturning toolboxes.
A park worker told The Times on Sunday: "We're not sure if the tourists who wanted to see them attack the cars provided them with weapons or if they themselves stole from pickups and vans".
They do indeed go through other people's toolboxes and take them with them everywhere. Someone has seen baboons dragging chainsaws.
Another staff member said: "Oh, we've seen people carrying knives and sticks. I wonder if the tourists handed them to them".
The baboon attack was visible, and even the mechanics in the north-west of England knew about the zoo.
A repairman in Greater Manchester said that people knew what was going on at the zoo and that two of his customers had been drugged this year.
They said that one minute the children wanted to see the monkeys getting into the cars, and the next minute, the license plates were gone, but such problems have brought good business to repair shops.
Northley Zoo insists that it is as safe as McDonald's, and that the zoo reopened on June 15 as a precautionary measure against the coronavirus outbreak as long as visitors stay in the car while visiting the entire five-mile wildlife park.
The zoo covers an area of about 222.7 hectares and feeds on animals such as lions, tigers, rhinos, horsemen and camels, who have been reported as possessing weapons.
A spokesperson said: "Well, we believe that many of these stories are exaggerated between word of mouth, and there are signs of oil spills that sometimes make finding objects in the park seem incredible.
In 2012, Hyundai, the car manufacturer, placed its new-generation i30 car in the zoo with 40 monkeys for 10 hours to prove the vehicle's strength sufficient to ensure the safety of families with children.
Manchester United, Chelsea to the Champions League, Bournemouth and Watford to the Premier League
On Sunday, Manchester United and Chelsea beat Leicester City on the final day of the Premier League to advance to the Champions League, while Bournemouth and Watford were relegated, ending a five-year top-flight rivalry.
After scoring Bruno Fernandes and Jesse Lingard, United beat Leicester City 2-0 and Ole Gunnar Solskjaer finished third.
Chelsea beat Wolves 2-0 with goals and an assist from Mason Mount, securing fourth place and overtaking Leicester City (which had been in the top four for much of the season) to claim the final spot in the UEFA Champions League.
Leicester City will compete in the Europa League, in addition to the Spurs, which tied Wolves 1-1 in the Crystal Palace match, ultimately beating Wolves due to a net winning margin.
To avoid the last-minute clash for the remaining two promotion spots, Aston Villa scored in the 84th minute and equalized one minute later with a goal from the substitute, leaving them tied at 1-1 with West Ham United.
Captain Jack Grealish scored a goal for Aston Villa, which could be his last match playing for his boyhood club.
Bournemouth defeated Everton 3-1, but finished one point behind Aston Villa.
Watford were relegated as runners-up after a 3-2 loss to Arsenal.
Manchester United scored more than 100 goals during the league season and won 5-0 against relegated Norwich, securing a place in the Europa League. This match was also David Silva's final Premier League game as he played for Manchester United for 10 years.
Kevin De Bruyne set a record this season with 20 assists and two goals.
Liverpool defeated Newcastle United 3-1 to end their title quest for this season, with the club ultimately scoring 99 points.
In addition, Southampton won 3-1 against Sheffield United and Danny Ings has scored 22 goals this season.
And Leicester City player Jamie Vardy scored the most goals and won the Golden Boot award.
Gwyneth Paltrow hopes that her children will develop good values.
Gwyneth Paltrow says that she tries to educate her children not only by teaching them to fish, but also by encouraging them to develop good values.
Paltrow and her ex-husband Chris Martin have two children, 16-year-old Apple and 14-year-old Moses, and she wants them to develop good values rather than relying on their parents' reputation.
“I am also the reason why I think ... the professional ethics of child-rearing, the importance of children's values to your own values or things that are consistent with their identity,” she told Rob Lowe in the podcast.
Well, the reality is that I can give you directions, but if you just try like everyone else, you're not going to get any results.
She adds, “As parents, what I can do for my children is teach them right from wrong and what work is. I did try to avoid just handing them fish.”
Paltrow has also taken steps to keep children's lives out of the public eye as much as possible and to ensure that their social media accounts remain private.
Given that there will be new elections, the Renewal Party finally agreed to join the coalition government.
Ted Cruz says Pelosi intends to use money to solve the problem and get the United States to shut down
Republican Senator Ted Cruz in Washington, Texas, accused House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and House Democrats of implementing the new coronavirus relief program, saying that the program was designed to divert money to solve the problem and put the United States at a standstill, and that consultations on follow-up measures were ongoing.
In an interview with Face the Nation on Sunday, Cruz said that the United States is facing two major crises: a global epidemic that has claimed more than 600,000 lives worldwide and an absolute economic catastrophe.
Cruz criticized Pelosi for not paying attention to any of these issues before he was interviewed for a nationally televised interview.
Cruz said her goal was to use money to solve problems and stop the United States.
You should especially look at the $3 trillion bill she is trying to push, which is nothing more than spending money without actually solving the problem. Our goal should be to get Americans back to work so they can support their families.
The Senate Republican Party and the White House consulted last week on the terms of the fourth phase of the programme to deal with the new crown epidemic, which is expected to reach $1 trillion.
At the end of last week, White House Office Director Mark Meadow and Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin returned to Congressional Hill for further discussions with Senate staff, and Mnuchin said in an interview with Fox News Channel on Sunday that Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell would reveal some of the details on Monday.
The most pressing issue facing lawmakers and the focus of the debate between Republicans and Democrats is that the increased unemployment benefits have expired and will be cancelled this weekend.
The Democrats want to keep the increased $600 grant in the fourth phase of the new coronavirus relief program, while the Republicans are calling for a cut, arguing that some Americans earn more than they earn from unemployment.
Cruz said that more than half of the U.S. population now receives higher unemployment benefits than they did when they were working, and he said he learned from conversations with small business owners in Tucson that some small business owners have had trouble recruiting.
He said: "Oh, they certainly won't come back to work, because the federal government pays them money that sometimes reaches twice their salary".
Cruz believes that Congress should focus on passing a recovery bill to ease taxes and regulations for small businesses so that Americans can return to work.
He said that the Housing Recovery Act does not bring down trillions of dollars, but suspends the wage tax, which is equivalent to paying every American who works. This is the only way to really get people back to work.
President Trump last week called for the next bailout to include wage tax cuts, but Mnuchin said the proposal would not be incorporated into the legislative process because the Republicans did not accept the proposal.
President Trump confirms that the next bill does not include a payroll tax cut, but attempts to blame it on the Democrats.
Last week, Cruz objected to the cost of the new proposal, stating on Sunday that he disagreed with the measure proposed by Senate Republicans.
However, he also criticized the Democratic-controlled House of Representatives' $3 trillion bill passed in May, accusing the other party of deliberately devising a measure that would harm the president's interests before the November election.
He said, "Democrat's only goal is to defeat Donald Trump, who naively thinks that the best way to defeat Donald Trump is to shut down all aspects of America and close every school in America."
Prime Minister Narendra Modi mourns Kargil heroes and says Pakistan is trying to stab India in the back.
Today is the 21st anniversary of the Kargil War, Prime Minister Narendra Modi said in his tribute to the martyrs of the Armed Forces that despite India's efforts to maintain friendly relations, Pakistan is still trying to stick its knife behind India's back.
In his monthly radio talk show, "Mann ki Baat", Prime Minister Modi said that Pakistan has been responsible for the disaster by devising an insidious plan to seize India's land and divert its domestic conflict.
He said that “Pakistan is shameless and attempts to occupy Indian territory in order to divert attention from internal turmoil.”
At that time, India was working to repair relations with Pakistan.
But, as it is said, the essence of evil is to fight with everyone for no reason.
People with this nature will only do evil, even if the other person wants to do something good for them.
He said, “This is why Pakistan ignored India's efforts to maintain relations and stabbed it behind its back.”
He added: "Imagine that the enemy is on a high hill and our army is fighting them below. But our high morale and real strength helped us defeat the enemy army in the mountains".
The courage of our armed forces enabled India to show great strength in the first battle of Kargil, Prime Minister Modi said, recalling what he had seen during his visit to the battlefield.
Every year, India holds an event to celebrate the victory of Kargil to commemorate the day when the Indian Army recaptured all Indian outposts in Kargil in Ladakh, which were occupied by Pakistani forces 21 years ago.
On July 26, 1999, the Indian Army announced the successful completion of Operation Vijay.
The war, which lasted for almost three months in the glacial highlands of Ladakh, resulted in the deaths of more than 500 Indian soldiers.
Earlier today, several senior officers and army leaders also visited the National War Memorial, Amar Jawan Jyoti, to pay tribute to the martyrs, including Defence Minister Rajnath Singh, Deputy Defence Minister Shripad Naik, Chief of Defence Staff Bipin Rawat, Army Chief of Staff M M Naravane, Air Force Commander R K S Bhadauria and Navy General Karambir Singh.
In the decades following the Kargil War, relations between India and Pakistan have been strained by a series of terrorist attacks.
India insists that negotiations and terrorist attacks cannot take place at the same time and Pakistan needs to destroy all terrorist camps operating within its territory.
Internet lending agencies warn: Small and medium-sized enterprises loan schemes may not reach their goals
Small and medium-sized enterprises are often not favored by large banks, so they increasingly meet their funding needs through online lending institutions.
However, Mr Callaghan said that internet lending institutions have no advantage over large banks because they do not have access to the same low-cost funds, and warned that SME guarantee schemes are not popular.
He said: "Well, I think it's very unlikely that this program will achieve its goal, and people are calling us and expecting very cheap money from us instead of half the loan from the government".
So they were very disappointed when they called us, because someone said they wanted to get a discount, and our financing costs didn't change at all.
Mr. Callaghan added that if fintech companies were able to get financing at a lower cost and then transfer the funds to small businesses, such a scheme would be easy to counter-manage.
“This is a bit of a dilemma — large banks have access to funds at a lower cost, but they prefer to avoid risk, so they will not actually lend money in the same way, they will not be able to access data, they will not feel safe unless you have a mortgage on your property”, he said, “Unless we can get cheaper money, I think the market has illusions about the popularity of this programme”.
Moula, based in Melbourne, was also involved in the development of the scheme, with CEO Aris Allegos saying that 50% of the guaranteed proceeds suggested in the government scheme should be given to small businesses, but different lenders would take different approaches.
He said: "Well, I can understand that others may not have experienced such a cost structure.
This may have something to do with their financial structure, what categories of entities they lend to, and how they assess the risk of these categories.
Mr. Allegos was reluctant to disclose how many loans Moula had specifically granted under the programme, but said that the revised programme “concentrated to reflect the changes that need to be made”, thus bringing greater flexibility to both borrowers and borrowers.
However, he is very concerned about the “two-speed economy” that is running and its impact on small businesses.
He said, “As far as small businesses are concerned, the biggest problem facing Moula and lending institutions is what you call cliffs and how to manage them.”
Overall, while there has been some growth in demand, it has not yet returned to pre-pandemic levels.
Prospa, an online lending company listed on the Australian Securities Exchange, also declined to disclose the amount of loans it issued under the scheme, saying it was business-sensitive information, but CEO Greg Moshal said the government's move to expand the scheme was welcomed.
He said: "We support the various enhancements made to the scheme, which enable small businesses to access a variety of financing products to meet their needs, including flexible short-term cash flow products.
Virginia Beach city demolishes Southern Alliance monument
Virginia Beach (VJ) - On Sunday, Virginia Beach City removed a 115-year-old South Alliance monument from the public area. Two days ago, the City Council unanimously decided to dismantle it.
According to media reports, workers dismantled the 27-foot (8.2-meter) high monument on Saturday morning.
The monument was erected in 1905 on the grounds of the original Princess Anne County Courthouse, where slave auctions also took place.
On Thursday evening, the city council unanimously decided to proceed with the related demolition work.
The city council listened to the views of dozens of speakers who believed that the monument meant a split for many and a constant reminder of the sad past.
Given the recent protests and destruction of several other monuments, the monument has now been completely covered up with locked doors around it.
The monument will be temporarily retained.
The city now needs to wait for a response from historical organizations or other relevant entities willing to deal with the monument.
Protests continue; police try to use new low-lethal tools
Violent clashes between law enforcement officers and protesters continue in Portland and across the country, sparking controversy over how to reduce the use of lethal weapons by the police.
Companies are increasing their efforts to provide new high-tech tools such as police remote-controlled handcuffs to replace more dangerous weapons such as pepper spray, rubber bullets and flash bullets.
Amnesty International, a human rights organization, analysed 500 videos of protests between 26 May and 5 June and found 125 cases of excessive use of force in 40 states and the District of Columbia.
Amnesty International's weapons investigator Brian Castner stated: “We have seen the misuse of tear gas, pepper spray, pepperball, 40 mm impact bombs, which are essentially a combination of low-lethal weapons in an inappropriate manner”.
Some people still hold the view that having a choice is important when it comes to avoiding the use of firearms.
Corey Pegues, former Deputy Superintendent of the New York Police Department, said: "Well, we should provide officers with all sorts of equipment, rather than using lethal weapons like guns, because police are the only profession in the United States that is empowered by citizens to take lives".
There is a new tool called Bola Wrap, a handheld device that launches an 8-foot Kevlar rope to wrap around a suspect.
Other new equipment includes long-range acoustic devices (also known as acoustic cannons), directed-energy weapons that use lasers to heat human skin, new shells made of materials such as foam and chalk powder, irritants, and electric shock guns.
Just days after George Floyd was killed by police, protests turned violent, and the stock price of gun manufacturer Axon surged over 18%, hitting an all-time high.
The total market for low-lethal weapons is expected to reach $118.5 billion by 2023.
Watch the video to learn about the new high-tech non-lethal tools used by police, how to regulate their use, and what risks these tools may pose to future policing and communities.
UN says thousands of Pakistani insurgents are stationed in Afghanistan
Islamabad — The United Nations reports that more than 6,000 Pakistani insurgents are hiding in Afghanistan, most of them illegal Pakistani Taliban groups, responsible for attacking Pakistani military and civilian targets.
According to a report released this week, the group known as Tehreek-e-Taliban (TTP) has ties to the Afghan branch of the Islamic State (IS).
Some TTP members have even joined IS affiliated organizations with a base in eastern Afghanistan.
On Sunday, the Afghan government did not respond to an Associated Press request for comment on related issues.
It was reported that IS in Afghanistan (i.e., in the province of Horasan) was hit hard by Afghan security forces and United States and NATO forces, and in some cases by the Afghan Taliban.
The report was written by the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Group, which is responsible for tracking terrorist organizations around the world.
The report estimates that there are 2,200 IS members in Afghanistan, although its leadership has been weakened, the group still views a Syrian citizen, Abu Said Mohammad al-Khorasani, as one of its leaders.
The report also mentions information from the Monitoring Group that two senior IS commanders, Abu Qutaibah and Abu Hajar al-Iraqi, arrived in Afghanistan from the Middle East.
The report notes that although ISIS-held territory is shrinking, they still have the capability to carry out attacks of some scale in various regions of the country, including Kabul.
The group is also trying to win over Taliban fighters who oppose signing an agreement with the US. The agreement in question refers to the peace deal signed between the US and the Taliban in February.
The aim of this agreement is to allow the United States to end its 19-year-long war in Afghanistan and to hope that the Taliban will guarantee that their territory will not be used by terrorist organizations.
The agreement is also expected to ensure that the Taliban are fully engaged in the fight against IS.
The second part of the agreement, perhaps the most important, called for talks between the Taliban and Kabul's political leaders.
Later on Saturday, the U.S. State Department issued a statement saying that its peace envoy Zalmay Khalilzad was still busy in the region, trying to push forward negotiations that had been repeatedly postponed due to disputes over the two sides' plans to release prisoners.
The United States-backed agreement called on the Afghan Government to release 5,000 Taliban prisoners and, accordingly, the Taliban to release 1,000 government and military personnel as olive branches to be thrown prior to negotiations between the two sides.
Currently, the Afghan government refuses to release about 600 of the Taliban prisoners, calling them high-profile criminals, but proposes to release only the remaining prisoners.
The Taliban rejected the government's proposal.
This is the closest opportunity in history for both sides to engage in internal negotiations and the next key step in ending the 40-year war in Afghanistan, the State Department said.
“Although important progress has been made in the exchange of prisoners, further efforts are needed to resolve the problem.”
Earlier this week, a political spokesman for the Taliban said that the Taliban planned to hold talks with political leaders in Kabul after the end of the monthly Ghoul-Bani (Islamic holiday) but on the condition that the release of the prisoners be completed first.
The biggest concern for Pakistan is the militants within Afghanistan, particularly those linked to the TTP, Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, and Lashkar-e-Islam, or the Balochistan Liberation Army, which launched large-scale attacks in southern Sindh and southwestern Balochistan that month.
Multiple Pakistani soldiers have been killed in clashes with militants in southwestern Balochistan province.
TTP launched an extremely horrific attack on Pakistan in 2014, which resulted in the death of 140 people at the Pakistan Army Academy.
Most of them are students, some as young as five years old.
According to the report, terrorist forces based outside Pakistan in Afghanistan threaten the security of the two countries, roughly estimating their numbers at 6,000 to 6,500, most of whom are members of the TTP.
HSBC, Britain's largest bank, denies setting up a trap for Chinese company Huawei in the US investigation – RT Business News
HSBC, a London-based multinational bank, said via social media that the bank had not fabricated evidence against Huawei as reported by Chinese media, leading to the arrest of Huawei's chief financial officer in Canada.
In a now-defunct WeChat post, the bank said that their actions did not prompt the US to investigate the world's largest provider of telecommunications network equipment, as China's scrutiny for alleged violations of US sanctions against Iran had already begun in the second half of 2016, long before the bank intervened.
The bank said on Saturday that HSBC had no hostility to Huawei and that there was no such thing as a Huawei trap, adding that it would never distort any facts about its customers.
In response to the information requested by the U.S. Department of Justice (US DOJ), HSBC Group merely stated objective facts.
HSBC has neither fabricated fake evidence nor concealed fake facts.
The platform blocked the article, suggesting that its content violated the rules of the site, which is no longer visible.
After multiple Chinese media outlets, including state-backed newspapers, accused HSBC of colluding with Washington to set up traps for Huawei, HSBC quickly issued this clarification.
In December 2018, Canadian police arrested Huawei's Chief Financial Officer Meng Wanzhou at the request of U.S. authorities, and for the next 18 months, the company remained a focal point of diplomatic news.
Ms. Meng faces fraud charges for allegedly using shell companies to violate sanctions against Iran and concealing Huawei’s relationship with Iranian company Skycom from banks.
The new evidence cited by The Global Times shows that HSBC had previously been very clear about the relationship between this Chinese company and Skycom.
The bank reportedly chose to collude with the U.S. government to avoid the harsher sanctions imposed in 2012, when HSBC was forced to admit money laundering and violated sanctions, including a ban on Iran.
At the time, the bank was ordered to pay a fine of about $1.9 billion and needed to take measures to prevent similar misconduct.
In an article about the incident, the paper wrote: "There is evidence that HSBC was well aware of this relationship, but deliberately sold its client's confidential business documents to the US, in order to get the US government to ignore the British bank's own misconduct and escape the punishment it deserves under the 2012 suspension of prosecution agreement".
The newspaper, which is controlled by the Chinese government, added in a public warning that it could be severely punished in one of HSBC's main markets if it confirms that banks have engaged in bribery.
In recent weeks, this well-known bank has been criticized several times.
Last month, HSBC was criticized for supporting the implementation of the new national security law in Hong Kong, which is the source of HSBC's business and contributes more than half of its profits.
HSBC is in two directions: in the West, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo called the bank "in bows"; and in the East, Chinese media have expressed support for the bank.
Angola: Pedalé Hospital transferred to Ministry of Health supervision
The President of the Republic of Rwanda, João Lourenço, decided on Friday to hand over control of the Pedro Maria Tonha Pedalé Hospital, under construction in Rwanda, to the Ministry of Health.
The press office of the President of the Republic said in a statement that the move was aimed at implementing the principles of government building, expanding the network of hospitals and ensuring better medical and pharmaceutical services for the public.
The document also mentions that “in the present exceptional circumstances, the measure enables the Angolan people to have a differentiated hospital of reference value equipped with modern technology and sophisticated instruments for the diagnosis and treatment of complex diseases”.
The message emphasizes that the decision of the President of Angola also takes into account the growth of the country's population, which drives the huge demand for the most diverse and high-quality healthcare services.
The document also notes that this requirement is justified because hospitals encompass the treatment, teaching, research and intervention of complex diseases related to ophthalmology and maternal-infant medicine, and that policies and management in these areas should fall within the responsibility of the Ministry of Health.
Algeria: President Tebboune mourns Mahmoud Ghamama – Algeria has lost a patriot and a steadfast warrior.
President of the Republic of Algeria Abdelmadjid Tebboune expressed his condolences to Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, a member of the National People's Congress who passed away on Friday, and said that Algeria is grieving the loss of a patriot, a fighter who dedicated himself to the cause of the people and country.
President Tebboune wrote in the hanging letter: "Today, Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama is gone, Algeria has lost a patriot, a warrior dedicated to the people and country, who has not wavered since the moment he took up arms, his determination is unwavering, and he fights alongside his brave jihadist brothers until he breathes his last breath on this sad day, when we can only obey the will of the Lord".
“The late Mahmoud Guema — may he rest in peace — was born in a special generation of patriots full of faith who died for their country.”
In 1960, at the peak of their lives, they joined the Tahart training camp in Wilaya VI, joined the revolutionary ranks, liberated Algeria from the villainous colonial grip, and sacrificed their lives.
The late leader had a fighting spirit dedicated to building a united and prosperous Algeria, and his name is thus widely known.
The President added, “In the far south, Mahmoud Guemama is a respected mediator. Once the spark of discord is ignited, he steps in to help his compatriots reach reconciliation.”
These qualities and values have earned Mahmoud Guemama the respect and trust of the people of Taman Rasset by promoting him as a multi-term representative of the people in the National People's Congress, an ambassador and defender of issues of concern to the people and the will of the people in the region, and a spokesperson for all the Algerian people.
No obituary will suffice to praise Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace.
Today, he left our mother when Algeria needed him most, the president said.
Finally, President Tebboune said: "I pray to Almighty Allah that the deceased may be bathed in the brightness of your holy mercy, in an immaculate paradise, to welcome him with the shuhadaa and the sunni".
I pray that Allah gives his family and loved ones patience and comfort… Only you have the power.”
Coronavirus Travel Guide: Countries Imposing Entry Restrictions on Bulgarian Passengers
More and more countries are implementing travel restrictions. Starting this week, the Czech Republic wants to test seasonal Bulgarian workers for coronavirus. Denmark stipulates that only Bulgarian citizens who are registered in the country and have employment contracts or business travel documents can enter the country.
Starting today, people who have visited Bulgaria and Romania in the past 14 days must undergo mandatory quarantine in Italy. Finland only accepts travelers with 'justifiable reasons' for entry.
Austria requires mandatory quarantine at home even if PCR test results are negative. Germany also implements the same policy for arrivals. For the next 14 days, entry is not permitted to southern Romania without sufficient reason. Greece has taken stricter measures on its land borders.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced that from Tuesday, people flying from Bulgaria to Greece will be required to show negative proof of the PCR test for the novel coronavirus to the Greek border authorities, which must be completed within 72 hours of entering their neighbours in southern Greece.
They also announced that travellers must have a corresponding test certificate to be allowed into the country. The certificate must be in English with the corresponding three names and numbers on the traveller's passport or identity card. If it does not meet the above requirements, the authorities of the southern neighbouring countries will not accept it.
Passengers are required to complete RT-PCR testing in the laboratory by extracting oral/nasal secretions. Accepted test results include results obtained after testing the novel coronavirus at a National Reference Laboratory, a National Health Laboratory or a private laboratory accredited by a relevant national accreditation body (not necessarily specifically for a novel coronavirus laboratory).
The measure does not apply to Greek citizens, citizens residing in Greece and travellers with significant professional reasons.
The Greek Ministry of Foreign Affairs also announced that travelers arriving in Greece on July 28 (Tuesday) and July 29 (Wednesday), who have not undergone testing prior to departure, will be tested upon arrival by the Greek authorities.
The following requirements remain valid: Passengers travelling by plane to Greece must complete an electronic version of the Passenger Location Form (PLF) generating a two-dimensional code at least 24 hours in advance and submit the form to the Greek authorities upon arrival in Greece. For more information see travel.gov.gr
Currently, there are no changes to the land border requirements for returning between Bulgaria and Greece. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs explains that passengers crossing the land border into Greece, regardless of nationality, must complete an electronic version of the passenger location form (PLF) to generate a two-dimensional code at least 24 hours before arrival.
The closure of Ilinden, Kapitan Petko Voyvoda and Makaza checkpoints led to increased traffic at the nearby Petrich border. There were no transit problems, no truck queues. All of them were waiting to leave Greece. Passengers had to wait about an hour and a half to cross.
There are three checkpoints open on the Bulgarian-Greek border. Only this part of Ilinden-Exohi and Ivaylovgrad is currently closed.
Dominic Cummings visits the SAS base and is taken hostage
The prime minister's chief adviser experienced the feeling of being held hostage when he visited the base last week.
For some harassed civil servants, the idea of holding Dominic Cummings hostage by pointing a gun at his head might have an appealing twist.
The hard-line Prime Minister's chief adviser visited the SAS headquarters in Stirling last week to experience a taste of the Middle East.
The Sunday Post revealed that part of his trip on Wednesday included a visit to the Counter-Terrorism Training House in the Counter-Terrorism Room, where elite soldiers honed their skills against terrorists.
He also visited the Haliford base under his guidance, inspected the military equipment of the regiment, and listened to briefings on recent operations, including efforts to wipe out the Islamic State terrorists in Iraq.
Significant visitors to the Stirling Defence Line have been particularly welcomed to experience the interior of the Anti-Terrorism Training Room, where SAS practices close-up combat in the two-story building.
Princess Diana had also visited here in 1983 during a training exercise when her hair was singed by the sparks from an accidentally thrown hand grenade.
It is understood that Mr Cummings arrived by helicopter and the base was named after Colonel David Stirling, the founder of the regiment.
He also visited the nearby Pontrilas military training area, where several other secretive units were stationed.
This visit is one stop on his tour of highly classified British security facilities, which has garnered attention from politicians and military leaders.
When news of its plans was leaked earlier in the month, Conservative backbencher and chairman of the House of Commons Defence Select Committee, Tobias Ellwood, said that MPs should have the same opportunities as ministers and civil servants to ask him about defence and security matters.
It is rare that the Special Committee requests the testimony of a special adviser appointed by a political figure.
It is understood that senior SAS officials used Wednesday's activities to highlight the importance of maintaining the number of Royal Marines and Airborne troops.
Accordingly, it has been previously reported that, in order to achieve cost savings, these units, which bring large numbers of personnel to the special forces, are likely to be downsized or merged.
According to a defence source, “SAS consists of 250 soldiers, more than half of whom were previously airborne, and the size of the Special Boat Squadron (SBS) is smaller and may not be sustainable without the support of the Royal Marine Corps”.
Mr Cummings was told that the units must be retained in their entirety.
Mr Cummings, who has complained that the military procurement pipeline continues to float billions of pounds, has recently attended meetings at MI5 and MI6 headquarters, and plans to visit the SBS headquarters in Poole, Dorsetshire, as well as the national top-secret laboratory in Boulton Down, Wiltshire.
The Ministry of Defense did not respond to a request for comment last night.
Sunday Morning Show: A Tribute to Rodgers and Hart
2020 marks the 100th anniversary of the debut of Richard Rodgers and Lorenz Hart, one of the most legendary musical theatre duo on Broadway.
To pay tribute to the creators of these two songs, Broadway stars Jelani Alladin (Cyclops, Titans) and Christiani Pitts (Golden Kong, Four Seas) and the dancer Richard Riaz Yoder (I Love Red Mother, Shuffle Long) co-executed a modern-style programme based on the original works of Rodgers and Hart, The Wandering Woman, which was also a special recording for the audience of Sunday morning.
Drummer Bryan Carter was the musical director and arranger for the show, with bassist Russell Hall performing alongside him.
Mountain fire controlled in Lassen County, California, U.S.
Lassen County in northeastern Sacramento County, California, has been hit by two wildfires that have burned an area of over 12,141 hectares and is still under intense firefighting efforts.
According to the California Fire Department, only 40 per cent of the “Gold” mountain fires in the southern part of the Aden area were under control as of Saturday morning, and large numbers of houses and pastures were still threatened by fires, which could have a significant impact on local agriculture, livestock and timber.
The fire has lasted for four days, burning approximately 8,498 hectares of land and seven houses, and injuring two firefighters who were sent to hospital.
Firefighters worked through the night to clear and reinforce the fire line under limited conditions.
But according to the weather forecast, it was expected to be windy and low humidity on that day.
The "Hog" wildfire that erupted in western Susanville has destroyed two buildings and approximately 4,047 hectares of land.
The California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection stated that the fire had been brought under control after firefighters worked through the night to clear and reinforce the fire line.
As of Saturday morning, the fire had been burning for six days and 45 percent of it was under control.
Beware of seemingly beautiful gardening tricks
It may be an embarrassing matter for botanists, but I am indeed very much addicted to gardening tricks.
On the bright side, I think of myself as an open-minded or somewhat irrational optimist, and many things in the botanical world are often beyond intuition.
But I am not ashamed to admit that I have stepped in numerous pits on such gardening tricks.
While I firmly believe in the value of trying new things, if I am not keen to open the “hell mode”, I can avoid some of the most common gardening tricks I have encountered below.
Firstly, perhaps the biggest minefield – grafting tomatoes onto potatoes.
Tomato grafting onto potatoes was hailed as a revolutionary breakthrough, though in fact it is an old-fashioned practice that was attempted as far back as the 1920s.
Tomatoes and potatoes belong to the same family; their internal “pipes” are roughly similar, so it is indeed possible to produce an enhanced miracle through grafting.
But the problem is that creating resource-intensive structures such as fruit and root crops requires a lot of energy for metabolism, and grafted plants need to meet the energy demands of two crops simultaneously, leaving limited energy to be diverted.
This means that the yield of these grafted plants is very low, quite different from what you see in the promotional pictures.
In addition, the time of ripening of the crop is different, and to harvest potatoes, the tomatoes that have not ripened must be crushed.
At the same time, propaganda images also imply a lot of very confusing tricks.
I've seen in some directories that PS handles blue flowers all day long, and some fruit trees produce an incredible number of fruits.
Some merchants even “borrow” photos from my Instagram account and falsely claim them to be their test bases on online shopping websites.
The actions of these few bad businessmen have forced you to look at these promotional images with a sceptical eye, especially images from the Internet.
If it looks too good to be true, it probably is.
When it comes to blue rose flowers, blue is relatively rare in the plant world, so if you see new varieties of colorful broom rashan, orchids, or cacti glowing with a blue light under high-concentration 'disinfectants,' it's essentially because they were sprayed with pesticide or dyed.
If suppliers explicitly state the situation, it's fine, but they rarely do so.
The blue butterfly orchid will revert to its original white, but cacti and holly ivy will quickly wilt after being coated with an anti-reflective dye.
Finally, I must expose the bonsai set that promises to grow miniature trees, and there are worse bonsai seeds than such sets.
Basin is not a plant.
This is a pruning technique reminiscent of ancient trees on distant mountains.
Basins are generally formed by cutting mature shrubs and then using lines to guide new growth directions.
I am aware that I will continue to be captivated by these horticultural tricks, which is one of the pleasures of experimentation.
But by avoiding these issues, you may have an opportunity to purchase a physical item that matches what the seller is displaying.
Spanish quarantine measures: Do you need to isolate in Spain?
No need, but if you return from Spain after midnight tonight, you must quarantine yourself at home for 14 days.
UK Attorney-General Humza Yousaf said: "Earlier this week we decided not to implement quarantine measures for people returning from Spain as the data showed an improvement in the spread of the virus.
But clearly, the latest data warrants our concern, and we therefore have to overturn the original decision.
We know it will be disappointing. But we have consistently made clear that we will closely monitor outbreaks in countries, and if the virus re-emerges in any country, we may ask to have it removed from the list of those not enforcing quarantine measures.”
The virus remains rampant and deadly. Our top priority is to contain the virus, prevent its spread, and protect public health.
The government has not yet announced the rules for British travel to Spain, but if you are planning to travel to Spain, it is best to check the regulations in your area.
Maria Jose Sierra, Deputy Director of Emergency Health in Spain, said:
"We have a major outbreak here. This could be the second wave. We need to pay attention to what is happening in the coming weeks."
Pakistan team Imam-Ul-Haq is still eligible to compete after injury
The Pakistan team and the England team will face each other in three Test matches and three T20Is.
Pakistan cricket player Imam-ul-Haq was injured when hit in the left hand during a team warm-up match in a derby, and the team doctor announced that he is now fit to play.
The Pakistan Cricket Board (PCB) stated in a statement that "the cricket player, who is known to use his left hand, participated in a warm-up match before the start of the next day's game."
On the first day of the match, Imam of the PCB White team and Naseem Shah of the PCB Green team were hit with their left hand.
On Friday evening, Imam suffered an injury to his left hand after being hit by a ball thrown by Shah and was forced to withdraw from the game, but he participated in the morning warm-up match and will return to the field on the third day.
More than 30 employees of a bank in Tamil Nadu have tested positive for COVID-19.
Officials from the government department of the Tiruchirappalli municipal corporation in Tamil Nadu and one state-owned bank said that at least 38 employees of a main branch of the bank located in Tiruchirappalli had tested positive for coronavirus.
Officials say they have suggested that customers who have visited the branch voluntarily participate in virus testing.
Prior to that, a senior staff member of the branch had other complications and later died of a new coronary pneumonia.
Officials said they recently conducted a large-scale medical screening of the branch, which resulted in the detection of these positive cases.
The branch manager said that they have completed disinfection and the bank may resume operations tomorrow.
Company health officials are in touch with customers who visited the bank.
Tamil Nadu is India's second most affected state in terms of confirmed cases, with Maharashtra having the highest number of infections. As of Saturday, the total number of confirmed cases reported in Tamil Nadu had reached 206,737.
India's Prime Minister will attend the groundbreaking ceremony for a temple built on a disputed site.
New Delhi (AFP) According to the Trust that oversees the construction of the temple, Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi is scheduled to attend the groundbreaking ceremony next month for the construction of the Hindu temple on the disputed site in northern India, which was originally a 16th-century mosque that was razed by Hindu fanatics in 1992.
The ceremony was scheduled to take place on 5 August, the day the organisers called the Day of Dajjal for Hindus, but it was also the anniversary of the Indian Parliament revoking its sole Muslim-majority semi-autonomous state of Jammu and Kashmir.
Both supporters and opponents of Modi's Hindu nationalist Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) cannot miss this symbolic day, which in its manifesto decades ago pledged to seize the autonomy of Kashmir and build a temple dedicated to the Hindu god Ram on the original site of a Mughal-era mosque in Ayodhya, Northeast India, believed by believers to be the birthplace of Ram.
The coronavirus is still spreading across India, which has reportedly become the third country with the highest number of cases globally, given that the ceremony will be broadcast live on national television, limiting the number of people and spectators attending the ceremony, according to Vishwa Hindu Parishad, a Hindu nationalist group allied with the Bharatiya Janata Party.
In a press conference on Saturday, the group's spokesman, Vinod Bansal, said that the temple will become a center of lasting social harmony, national unity and fusion, awakening the characteristics of Hinduism.
Following the overwhelming victory of the Bharatiya Janata Party in the election, a century-long dispute over the site was resolved last year.
In November, the Supreme Court ruled in favor of the temple trust, saying that he would instead allocate five acres of land to Muslim petitioners.
Hindu fanatics have long claimed that Mughal Muslim invaders built a mosque on top of the existing temple in the ancient city of Ayutthaya.
The riots that followed the demolition of the mosque in December 1992 triggered community violence, killing about 2,000 people, mostly Muslims.
Meanwhile, the special court is still hearing the case of the mosque demolition.
An architect from Modi's hometown of Ahmedabad, Gujarat, proposed building a towering sandstone structure with a height of 161 feet (49 meters) with five towers.
